for [1,2,3,4] → 3\n```\n\nThis returns 3 for `[1, 2, 3, 4]`, but the test expects 2.5 (the average of the two middle values).\n\n## Solution Implemented\nI updated the `median()` function to properly handle both odd and even-length lists:\n```python\ndef median(values):\n    if not values:\n        raise ValueError(\"median() requires at least one value\")\n    ordered = sorted(values)\n    n = len(ordered)\n    mid = n // 2\n\n    if n % 2 == 1:\n        # Odd number of elements: return the middle one\n        return ordered[mid]\n    else:\n        # Even number of elements: average the two middle ones\n        return (ordered[mid - 1] + ordered[mid]) / 2\n```\n\n## Results\nAll 4 tests now pass:\n- ✅ `test_mean`: PASSED\n- ✅ `test_median_odd`: PASSED\n- ✅ `test_median_even`: PASSED (fixed!)\n- ✅ `test_median_does_not_mutate`: PASSED\n\nThe `median()` function now correctly implements the standard statistical definition:\n- For odd-length lists: returns the middle element\n- For even-length lists: returns the average of the two middle elements\n\nThe fix maintains backward compatibility for odd-length lists while correcting the behavior for even-length lists as specified in the test suite.","ttft_ms":28934,"ttft_stream_ms":26002,"time_to_request_ms":75,"type":"result","duration_ms":56012,"uuid":"8dcf2e50-deea-48e5-a27f-d6f782c28453","queued_turn_count":0}
